    {
      "ts": 1790898110.379669,
      "status": "done",
      "result": {
        "summary": "All four requested fixes are in place. (1) bus.read validates each id with re.fullmatch(r\"T-[0-9]+\", ...) before get(), counts a non-match as skipped, and catches KeyError, OSError, ValueError (JSONDecodeError is a ValueError subclass) and TypeError. It also skips rows that are not dicts or lack id/status/role. (2) The ids path uses sorted(set(ids)), so the order is ascending and duplicates are removed. (3) test_bus_read_parent_and_ids_filters captures stderr and asserts the notice for 1 unreadable row on T-9999. It checks out-of-order ids with a duplicate, asserts that ids=['../../etc/passwd'] returns [] without calling get(), and adds malformed-file cases. (4) bus_mcp.bus_read coerces read_warn_rows with int() inside the try block and falls back to 200; the except clause covers AttributeError, TypeError and ValueError. test_bus_read_unfiltered_notice_above_warn_rows covers the 'x' value, and test_bus_create_task_echoes_compact_row is still present. I did not run the tests or the gate. One minor note: the T-[0-9]+ check also applies to the unfiltered DB path, which now skips any row whose id doesn't match. next_id always generates T-NNNN, so this is safe today.",
        "verdict": "approve",
        "comments": [
          {
            "path": "orchestrator/bus.py",
            "line": 205,
            "issue": "The id regex also filters the unfiltered DB-scan path, so a row whose id doesn't match T-<digits> is silently counted as skipped. This is harmless while next_id only emits T-NNNN. Consider limiting the regex to the caller-supplied ids branch.",
            "severity": "low"
          }
        ],
        "packet_version": "5b3128940458",
        "confidence": 0.0,
        "provenance": [
          "repo"
        ]
